    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The ability for block copolymers (BCPs) to spontaneously self-assemble into a wide range of nanostructured morphologies has received considerable attention for their technological applications. To expand the range of nanostructures accessible to block copolymers, we design dynamic block copolymers (DBCPs) that combine the phase separation of traditional block copolymers with the supramolecular self-assembly of periodic dynamic polymers to achieve the spontaneous formation of supramolecular nanofibers with well-ordered phase domains. These DBCPs are multiblock copolymers, meaning they consist of multiple oligomers of each block type covalently connected by dynamic hydrogen bonding units. We prepared DBCPs in which the oligomer blocks are either connected in a statistically random sequence or in a periodically alternating sequence. In this work, we investigate how the block sequence affects the supramolecular nanofiber self-assembly, bulk mechanical properties, thermal phase behavior, and ionic conductivity of the DBCPs. We demonstrate that the DBCPs with a periodic block sequence self-assemble into supramolecular nanofibers with well-ordered PEG and PDMS domains, in contrast to those with a random sequence which do not form nanofibers but have disordered morphologies. The periodicity of the block sequence ensures the regularly spaced placement of the dynamic bonds along the polymer chain, which enables the facile stacking of the hydrogen bonding units into ordered 1D supramolecular nanofibers, which delays the onset of terminal flow by up to ~60 °C compared to the random block sequence. The hierarchically assembled supramolecular nanofibers display complex phase behaviors as a result of both block copolymer phase segregation and supramolecular interactions. Despite identical chemical composition, the periodic DBCPs demonstrate bulk ionic conductivity values almost an order of magnitude higher than their random counterparts due to the formation of nanofibers with well-ordered, interconnected, ion-transporting PEG domains.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
